encyclopedia :signal_fed do
  name "The Signal-Fed"
  summary "The Signal-Fed are sessile and slow-moving organisms that convert signal-band emission into heat and mineral growth. Loop-feeders, leak-grazers, and burst-crusts colonize different repeating faults in the broken network."
  kind :lifeform
  subkind :ecological_group
  status :complete
  log "2026-08-31 — Renamed The Bandfed to The Signal-Fed; the established signal root names the specific resonance band that defines the group's metabolism."
  topics :ecology, :"signal-freq", :orbital
  prevalence :uncommon
  appears_when all: { place: [:damaged_signal] }
  origin "Documented as ring-era vermin in the network's maintenance records; the broken net's standing faults turned a scoured pest into an established family"
  biology "Sessile and slow-moving grazers — shelled clusters, crusts, and stalked fans — whose tissues couple to signal-band emission and convert the carried energy to heat and mineralized shell; growth tracks the feeding signal's strength and repetition"
  lifespan "Colonies persist as long as their signal does — the great shells on the oldest stuck repeaters have been growing since the Famine"
  function "Living fault survey: colonies mark where signal is leaking, stuck, or looping, their size dates the fault, and their shells record its history in growth bands the relay trades read like cores"
  resonance_relation "Direct feeding on signal-band emission — the family's whole definition — with each species tuned to its preferred repetition patterns: the loop-feeders on stuck repeaters, the leak-grazers along cracked line, the burst-crusts at intermittent faults"

  descriptive_identity(
    appearance: "Pale mineral growth where clean fabric should be — banded " \
                "shell clusters on a repeater housing, crust runs along a " \
                "cracked line, stalked fans spread toward a leaking joint — " \
                "warm to the touch in cold country, which is the feeding " \
                "made visible.",
    behavior: "Colonies settle where signal repeats, grow with the feeding, " \
              "and hold station for as long as the fault does; a repaired " \
              "line starves its colony on the repair's own schedule, and " \
              "the dying shell's last band records the fix.",
    threat: "A heavy colony can consume the remaining traffic on a weak line, " \
            "and accumulated shell mass can crack a repeater housing or close " \
            "an access panel.",
    senses: "Each form couples to a range of signal strength and repetition. " \
            "Loop-feeders settle on repeating housings, leak-grazers extend " \
            "along damaged line, and burst-crusts grow during intermittent " \
            "transmission.",
    risks: "Mineral growth bands preserve changes in signal strength, long " \
           "interruptions, and some repair attempts. Reading them requires a " \
           "local sample and comparison with the line's known materials."
  )

  prose <<~PROSE
    The Signal-Fed include shelled clusters, creeping crusts, and stalked fans whose tissues couple to signal-band emission. They convert the energy into heat and mineral growth. Membership follows this metabolism across several ancestral lines: loop-feeders settle on housings repeating the same traffic, leak-grazers extend along cracked line, and burst-crusts add growth during intermittent transmission. Organisms living near relays without feeding on the signal fall outside the group.

    Ring maintenance records classify these organisms as network vermin and describe regular scouring. Persistent postfall faults gave them stable habitat. A loop-feeder fixes to a repeater and builds a banded shell around the emission source. A leak-grazer advances slowly along the damaged section as new growth settles ahead of the old crust. Stalked forms turn their fans toward a failing joint. Active colonies are warm in cold country; when the signal ends, growth stops and the exposed tissue recedes behind mineral shell.

    Line crews use settlement shape to locate faults and cut shell sections to read growth bands. Band width tracks signal strength; gaps mark interruptions; a final thin edge can date a repair. Shell mass also loads weak lines and can crack housings, so working relays are cleared before a colony becomes a landmark. Great loop shells remain around some abandoned Famine-era repeaters, still warm wherever the old message continues.
  PROSE

  cue "The line crew walks the buried run with eyes on the ground — the leak-grazers' pale crust creeping along the surface traces the crack beneath, meter for meter, ahead of any excavation."
  cue "The shell-reader cuts a section from the repeater's barrel-thick colony, reads the bands by lamp — steady growth, two thin famine lines, one repair attempt — and dates the fault to a decade the registry's records had lost."
  affordance "Colonies are a living fault survey — location by settlement, age by size, history by growth band — and the shell-readers' cut sections settle disputes about when a line failed and who was maintaining it at the time."
  variation "Loop-feeders build the great banded shells on stuck repeaters; leak-grazers run in crusts along cracked line; burst-crusts colonize intermittent faults and grow in the family's strangest stop-start banding."
  variation "New forms are recorded when a novel fault pattern supports a distinct feeding cycle. Relay crews assign a working name first; later surveys determine whether the form belongs to an existing lineage."
end
